{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To find the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) such that \\((ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1)\\) is divisible by \\(x^2 + 1\\), we need to ensure that the polynomial \\((ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1)\\) evaluates to zero when \\(x\\) is a root of \\(x^2 + 1\\). The roots of \\(x^2 + 1 = 0\\) are \\(x = i\\) and \\(x = -i\\), where \\(i\\) is the imaginary unit.\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\(x = i\\) into the polynomial:\n\\[\n(ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1) = (ai + b)((i)^5 + 1) - (5i + 1).\n\\]\nSince \\(i^5 = i\\), we have:\n\\[\n(ai + b)(i + 1) - (5i + 1) = (ai + b)(i + 1) - 5i - 1.\n\\]\nExpanding \\((ai + b)(i + 1)\\), we get:\n\\[\n(ai + b)(i + 1) = ai^2 + ai + bi + b = -a + ai + bi + b = -a + b + (a + b)i.\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n-a + b + (a + b)i - 5i - 1 = (-a + b - 1) + (a + b - 5)i.\n\\]\nFor this to be zero, both the real part and the imaginary part must be zero. Therefore, we have the system of equations:\n\\[\n-a + b - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":955,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,1477,279,11457,6716,1124,1188,64,11,293,10699,8,1741,429,1124,1188,706,488,293,2376,87,61,20,488,220,16,8,481,320,20,87,488,220,16,10699,8,374,74916,553,17767,87,61,17,488,220,16,59,701,582,1184,311,5978,429,279,47311,1124,1188,706,488,293,2376,87,61,20,488,220,16,8,481,320,20,87,488,220,16,10699,8,66249,311,7168,979,17767,87,57758,374,264,3704,315,17767,87,61,17,488,220,16,59,568,576,19703,315,17767,87,61,17,488,220,16,284,220,15,57758,525,17767,87,284,600,57758,323,17767,87,284,481,72,59,701,1380,17767,72,57758,374,279,49952,4982,382,5338,11,1077,594,27679,17767,87,284,600,57758,1119,279,47311,510,59,9640,41922,488,293,2376,87,61,20,488,220,16,8,481,320,20,87,488,220,16,8,284,320,2143,488,293,14362,72,29776,20,488,220,16,8,481,320,20,72,488,220,16,4