 numbers for which\n\\[(ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1)\\]is divisible by $x^2 + 1.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ca37bc070811e618706a8db2765ffeb4c96191d7313352f7e1fb6dd37682b148"},"seconds":22.68137788772583,"seed":230371704034294287,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) such that \\((ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1)\\) is divisible by \\(x^2 + 1\\), we need to ensure that the polynomial \\((ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1)\\) evaluates to zero when \\(x\\) is a root of \\(x^2 + 1\\). The roots of \\(x^2 + 1 = 0\\) are \\(x = i\\) and \\(x = -i\\), where \\(i\\) is the imaginary unit.\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\(x = i\\) into the polynomial:\n\\[\n(ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1) = (ai + b)((i)^5 + 1) - (5i + 1).\n\\]\nSince \\(i^5 = i\\), we have:\n\\[\n(ai + b)(i + 1) - (5i + 1) = (ai + b)(i + 1) - 5i - 1.\n\\]\nExpanding \\((ai + b)(i + 1)\\), we get:\n\\[\n(ai + b)(i + 1) = ai^2 + ai + bi + b = -a + ai + bi + b = -a + b + (a + b)i.\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n-a + b + (a + b)i - 5i - 1 = (-a + b - 1) + (a + b - 5)i.\n\\]\nFor this to be zero, both the real part and the imaginary part must be zero. Therefore, we have the system of equations:\n\\[\n-a + b - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's substitute \\(x = -i\\) into the polynomial:\n\\[\n(ax + b)(x^5 + 1) - (5x + 1) = (a(-i) + b)((-i)^5 + 1) - (-5i + 1).\n\\]\nSince \\((-i)^5 = -i\\), we have:\n\\[\n(-ai + b)(-i + 1) - (-5i + 1) = (-ai + b)(-i + 1) + 5i - 1.\n\\]\nExpanding \\((-ai + b)(-i + 1)\\), we get:\n\\[\n(-ai + b)(-i + 1) = a i^2 - ai - bi + b = a - ai - bi + b = a + b - (a + b)i.\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\na + b - (a + b)i + 5i - 1 = (a + b - 1) + (5 - a - b)i.\n\\]\nFor this to be zero, both the real part and the imaginary part must be zero. Therefore, we have the system of equations:\n\\[\na + b - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\n5 - a - b = 0 \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nNotice that equations (1) and (3) are the same, and equations (2) and (4) are the same. Solving equations (1) and (2):\n\\[\n-a + b - 1 = 0 \\implies b = a + 1\n\\]\n\\[\na + b - 5 = 0 \\implies a + (a + 1) - 5 = 0 \\implies 2a + 1 - 5 = 0 \\implies 2a - 4 = 0 \\implies 2a = 4 \\implies a = 2.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 2\\) into \\(b = a + 1\\), we get:\n\\[\nb = 2 + 1 = 3.\n\\]\n\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(2, 3)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(2, 3)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:49"}
